\title{Arrow interoperability}
\description{
\code{decimal} vectors convert to and from Arrow decimal arrays exactly, in both
directions, when the arrow package is installed.

\strong{Arrow to decimal.} \code{\link[=as_decimal]{as_decimal()}} accepts an Arrow \code{Array} or
\code{ChunkedArray}. A \code{decimal32()}, \code{decimal64()}, \code{decimal128()} or
\code{decimal256()} array converts exactly, taking its scale from the Arrow
type. An integer array of any width converts exactly too. Other Arrow
types convert to the equivalent R vector first and follow the ordinary
\code{\link[=as_decimal]{as_decimal()}} rules. \code{\link[=arrow_as_data_frame]{arrow_as_data_frame()}} applies the same conversion
to every decimal field of a \code{Table} or \code{RecordBatch}.

\strong{Decimal to Arrow.} A \code{decimal} vector becomes a decimal field wherever
arrow infers types: \code{arrow::as_arrow_array()}, \code{arrow::arrow_table()},
\code{arrow::write_parquet()} and \code{arrow::write_dataset()}. By default the field
is an Arrow \emph{extension type} whose storage is a real \code{decimal128()} or
\code{decimal256()} with the vector's scale and a precision inferred from the
values. Other readers see the storage, a plain decimal column. In R the
column returns as a \code{decimal} vector on every read path, \code{as.data.frame()},
\code{arrow::read_parquet()} and \code{dplyr::collect()} included.
\code{\link[=arrow_decimal_type]{arrow_decimal_type()}} pins a precision and scale.

Arrow's compute engine does not operate on extension columns, so arrow-side
arithmetic or filtering on such a column fails. To write a plain field
instead, pass a plain Arrow decimal type as \code{type} to
\code{arrow::as_arrow_array()}, or set the option below. A plain field comes
back from arrow's own conversion as a \code{double} wearing the \code{decimal} class,
because arrow reapplies the column's recorded R attributes; the package
refuses to format such an object. Read those tables with
\code{\link[=arrow_as_data_frame]{arrow_as_data_frame()}}.

Infinities and NaNs have no Arrow decimal representation and raise an error
on conversion to Arrow. Arrow accepts a negative scale but Parquet does not,
so rescale such a vector with \code{as_decimal(x, scale = 0)} before
\code{arrow::write_parquet()}.
}
\section{Options}{

\code{decimal.arrow_extension}: \code{TRUE} (the default) writes the extension type;
\code{FALSE} writes plain decimal fields everywhere.
